!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Metadata
description: a JSON-Schema-equivalent written in yamlover — typing, decoding, presentation
- |
  yamlover keeps a schema, repurposed: a **JSON-Schema-equivalent written in yamlover** —
  the same or close vocabulary (`properties`, `type`, `format`, `prefixItems`, `items`) —
  whose primary purpose is **metadata**: typing, decoding, and presentation, with
  validation a secondary, optional use
  ([metadata first](::language:principles:metadata-first)). Data lives in the instance;
  the schema only describes it. Schema-as-storage — pinning data with `const` so the
  schema *is* the instance — is the one old model that was dropped.
- |
  **Two ways to attach.** A directory's `.yo/meta.yo` overlay nests under the JSON-Schema
  keywords, so the engine maps a meta-path to an instance-path (`properties: age`
  annotates the instance node `age`). In a file, the `!!<…>` tag attaches a schema to the
  value that follows it — or, alone at the top, to the document root. The tag's contents
  are themselves yamlover: a pointer to a hosted schema, or an inline schema literal —
  a one-off format needs no named definition. json5p has no tags: overlay only.
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  !!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>     # a pointer to a hosted schema
  My Article
  - !!<format: text/x-plantuml> |   # an inline schema literal — itself
    @startuml                       #   a one-line yamlover/meta document
    …
- |
  **What the metadata drives.** *Decoding*: a leaf's type and format say how to turn
  bytes into a value — `type: binary` with `format: int32/le` decodes a four-byte file —
  and `format` also names a sub-document encoding (`yamlover`, `json5p`, or a `…/meta`
  variant for a schema document). *Rendering*: the viewer picks a renderer from the
  facets and the format. *Presentation*: `title` and `description` annotate a node
  without living in its data. Format resolution order: the meta `format:` if present;
  else a recognized file extension; else — no meta and no extension — `binary`.
- |
  **The yamlover specifics** over JSON Schema: `type` adds `binary`, `mixed` (the schema
  name of the `!!mix` shape), and `variant` (the omni value-plus-fields shape, the
  self-value given as `value:`); `format` is open — a MIME type, a codec, or a
  yamlover-defined view;
  `uniqueItems: true` marks a set (the overlay-and-json5p route to `!!set`); `concrete`
  may be stated but is normally inferable from the filesystem — keep meta minimal.
  References use `*` pointers, not `$ref`: reusable fragments live under `$defs` and are
  referenced with a normal pointer — one reference mechanism for the whole language. The
  combinators (`anyOf`, `oneOf`, `allOf`, `not`, type unions) are kept: a heterogeneous
  positional body is an `items` union, and `allOf` over a schema pointer is the
  provisional "extends".
- |
  **Hosted schemas.** The yamlover project hosts reusable definitions under `$defs` at
  its project root, and the engine grafts the self-import key `yamlover` into every
  served root — so `!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>` resolves from any project, a detached
  tree included (the taxonomy ships as package data). The document-model schemas hosted
  there — `chunk`, `chapter`, `table` — are specified in the
  [documents chapter](::documents).
- |
  A served root's `.yo/settings.yo` carries project configuration. The governing rule:
  **settings are defaults, never constraints** — a node is identified by what it *is*,
  not by where it sits; a setting only says where to create things when the user
  doesn't.
